      "text": "To find the smallest two-digit integer \\( n \\) such that switching its digits and then adding 3 results in \\( 2n \\), we start by expressing \\( n \\) in terms of its digits. Let \\( n = 10a + b \\), where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the digits of \\( n \\), with \\( a \\) being the tens digit and \\( b \\) being the units digit. When we switch the digits, the number becomes \\( 10b + a \\). According to the problem, switching the digits and then adding 3 gives us \\( 2n \\). Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[\n10b + a + 3 = 2(10a + b)\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying the right side of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n10b + a + 3 = 20a + 2b\n\\]\n\nRearranging the terms to isolate \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) on one side, we have:\n\n\\[\n10b + a + 3 - 2b = 20a\n\\]\n\\[\n8b + a + 3 = 20a\n\\]\n\\[\n8b + 3 = 19a\n\\]\n\nThis equation tells us that \\( 8b + 3 \\) must be a multiple of 19. We need to find the smallest positive integer values for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) that satisfy this equation. We will test successive values of \\( b \\) starting from the smallest possible value.\n\nIf \\( b = 1 \\):\n\\[\n8(1) + 3 = 11 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 19)}\n\\]\n\nIf \\( b = 2 \\):\n\\[\n8(2) + 3 = 19 \\quad \\text{(a multiple of 19)}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\( a = 1 \\). Therefore, the two-digit number \\( n \\) is \\( 10a + b = 10(1) + 2 = 12 \\). We verify this solution by switching the digits and adding 3:\n\n\\[\n12 \\rightarrow 21 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 21 + 3 = 24\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\times 12 = 24\n\\]\n\nThe equation holds true, so the smallest two-digit integer \\( n \\) is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
